        {
          "subsection_id": "thoracocentesis_kit_subsection_18",
          "subsection_title": "Common OSCE questions and model answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "thoracocentesis_kit_group_19",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is thoracocentesis?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is a procedure in which a needle or catheter is used to access the pleural space to obtain a diagnostic sample or remove fluid for therapeutic purposes."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the commonest diagnostic indication?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Investigation of a pleural effusion when fluid analysis is clinically indicated."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Why is ultrasound important?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It helps locate the fluid, identify the diaphragm and nearby structures, and select a safer aspiration site."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Name four complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Pneumothorax, bleeding, infection and organ injury. Re-expansion pulmonary oedema is another important complication."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. How does thoracocentesis differ from chest-tube drainage?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Thoracocentesis is usually a single aspiration procedure, whereas a chest tube connected to a drainage system is used when ongoing drainage is required."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What investigations can be performed on pleural fluid?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Depending on the indication, these may include cell count and differential, protein, lactate dehydrogenase, glucose, pH, microbiology and cytology."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What would you do if the patient develops chest pain and breathlessness during aspiration?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Stop aspiration, assess the patient immediately and arrange urgent clinical review. Investigate for complications such as pneumothorax or re-expansion pulmonary oedema as appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. Why must specimens be labelled correctly?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Correct labelling preserves patient identification and ensures the laboratory performs and interprets the requested tests accurately."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
